{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And they saw him, etc.</b> One needs to understand the verse's saying <b>and while still</b>, etc. — whether it is a continuation of what precedes it, in this manner: ״And they saw him from afar, before he drew near to them,״ or whether it is the beginning of the narration of what follows it, in this manner: ״Before he drew near, etc., they conspired against him,״ etc.? And on either side it is difficult: if it attaches to what precedes it, it should have said ״before he drew near״; and if it attaches to what follows it, it should have said ״and while still, etc., they conspired.״ And perhaps it is that he intended by the added ו to intensify the distance — meaning, that you should not think the distance was merely that he had not yet come close to them, but rather ״and while still he had not drawn near״ — they saw him at an extreme distance." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend in this manner: <b>And they saw him from afar</b> — meaning: from the distance of the heart; they did not see him with the sight of brothers toward their brother, but rather as a man far removed from them. <b>And while still he had not drawn near</b>, etc. — meaning: and this seeing was also ״before,״ etc., and had he not said ״and while still״ with the added ו, it would have been understood that this is the meaning of ״from afar״ that he stated; therefore he said it with the added ו to indicate that it is a different matter. And there is yet another way to settle the verse, except that this one seems more fitting."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:46:36.531206616Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}